'''Anthomyidæ''' (an-thō-mī′i-dē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''Anthomyia'' + ''-idæ;'' also in the perverted form ''Anthomyzidæ'' (''Anthomyzides'', Latreille).] In some systems of classification, a family of dipterous insects, corresponding more or less exactly to the ''Anthomyzides'' of Latreille: sometimes merged in ''Muscidæ''.

'''Anthomyza''' (an-thō-mī′zä), ''n.'' [NL., a perverted form for ''Anthomyia.''] '''1.''' In ''entom.'': (''a'') Same as ''Anthomyia''. ''Fallen'', 1810. (''b'') A genus of lepidopterous insects. ''Swainson'', 1833.—'''2.''' In ''ornith.'', a genus of meliphagine birds, whose type is ''A. cæruleocephala'' of New Zealand, named by Swainson in 1837. The name, being preoccupied in entomology, was changed to ''Anthornis'' by G. R. Gray in 1840.

'''Anthomyzidæ''' (an-thō-mī′zi-dē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''Anthomyza'', 1, + ''-idæ.''] Same as ''Anthomyidæ''.

'''Anthomyzides''' (an-thō-mī′zi-dēz), ''n. pl.'' [NL., prop. F. pl., equiv. to ''Anthomyzidæ.''] In Latreille’s system of classification, a subtribe of ''Muscides'', corresponding closely to ''Anthomyidæ''. {{smaller|It is composed of species having the appearance of common flies, with 4-jointed abdomen, non-vibratile wings, and short antennæ ending in a long or linear joint,}}

